  "query_preview": "(Today through Monday)...\nIssued at 312 AM EDT Tue Oct 20 2026\n\nLovely autumn weather will continue to be the rule through the seven \nday forecast period, courtesy of broad, quasi-stationary ridging.\nSurface high pressure that has occupied much of the eastern two-\nthirds of North America..will be maintained from the MS Valley to \nAtlantic coast...while corresponding upper ridging builds across \nthe continent, extending from central Mexico to eastern Canada by \nthe end of the week.\n\nMeanwhile in central Indiana, the lack of waves from the retracted \njet will keep light to calm winds, which should display an overall \nveering from NE to SE this week as the surface ridge axis slides \nfrom the Great Lakes to the Appalachians.  The building upper \nridge's proximity from just west, to over the local region, will \nprovide strong subsidence, maintaining an overall dry column.  The \nonly possibly impactful weather will be early morning fog, which \nwould be favored in/near valleys along rivers and creek beds.\n\nFor latter portions of the period, an increasingly active northern \njet stream will likely host a potent baroclinic system over central \nCanada this weekend.  However, the only effects on the CWA through \nnext Monday would be a more pronounced breeze ahead of the system's \ndragging / quasi-stationary cold front. High confidence in no \nprecipitation through this weekend with the staunch surface ridge \nadjusting its alignment over the Deep South, blocking any \norganized return of Gulf moisture...and most likely directing any \nmoderate rainfall with the northern system's front closer to \nWI/MI. Potential for a transition to colder conditions by the \nmiddle of next week.",
